@* How a spirit's games split across the four terror levels, as one small stacked bar. The ramp
   is TerrorScale's, shared with the distribution panel's tiles, so the two climb the same colours.

   The track is .meter's, the same one every other proportion bar in the app stands in; only the
   fill differs, and it differs enough to stay here rather than in Meter: this is four segments
   laid side by side, not one width against a rest. Standing outside Meter is also how it came to
   be the one bar still writing its widths in whatever culture the server happens to run under.

   Its own width is w-28 rather than a parameter: one table draws this, and the parameter's
   default had already stopped being the width in use. *@

@{
    var total = TerrorScale.Order.Sum(Get);
    if (total == 0)
    {
        @* .no-value — the register for a value that isn't there, as the tables' own dashes are.
           The size is this cell's, which the class leaves to the caller. *@
        <span class="no-value text-[10px]">no data</span>
        return;
    }
}

<div class="meter flex h-2 w-28">
    @foreach (var t in TerrorScale.Order)
    {
        var count = Get(t);
        if (count == 0) continue;
        @* The width is going into CSS, so it is spelled invariantly — see BrowserNumber. *@
        var pct = Percent.Of(count, total).ToInvariant("F2");
        <div @key="t" class="h-full" style="width:@(pct)%; background-color: @TerrorScale.Color(t);"
             title="@TerrorScale.Label(t): @Copy.Count(count, "game")"></div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyDictionary<TerrorLevel, int> Counts { get; set; } =
        new Dictionary<TerrorLevel, int>();

    private int Get(TerrorLevel t) => Counts.TryGetValue(t, out var c) ? c : 0;
}
